<h2>The Complete Guide to Renting a 17 Seater Urbania in Bangalore</h2>
<p>This guide is written for anyone planning a trip for 13 to 17 people from Bangalore: a wedding party, a pilgrimage group, a company offsite, a family reunion or a friends' holiday. It explains what the 17 seater Urbania is, how to seat and pack a full group, what each type of trip needs, how the pricing works with real worked examples, and how to avoid the mistakes that cost large groups money and time. Every rate quoted here is the live Yogi Tours &amp; Travels tariff: ₹38 per km, a 300 km daily minimum and ₹700 per day driver Bata, with tolls, parking, permits and state taxes billed at actuals. If you only want the price, jump to the <a href="#urbania-fares">fare table</a>. If you want to plan the whole trip, read on.</p>

<h2>Part 1. Understanding the 17 Seater Urbania</h2>

<h3>What the Force Urbania actually is</h3>
<p>The Force Urbania is a large passenger van built by Force Motors. Most travellers know Force vehicles through the older Traveller, the long-running van that has carried groups across India for decades. The Urbania is a different design. It is built on a monocoque body, which means the body and frame are one structure instead of a body bolted onto a separate ladder frame. For a passenger, the practical result is a ride that feels closer to a large car than to a commercial van: less body roll, less vibration through the floor and a noticeably quieter cabin. The roof is higher, so most adults can stand and walk down the aisle without stooping.</p>
<p>That matters most on long days. A group travelling from Bangalore to Ooty or Wayanad spends six to seven hours on the road. In a vehicle that shakes and drones, those hours are tiring, and elderly relatives and children feel it first. In an Urbania the cabin stays calmer, which is why this vehicle has become the first choice for families that have the budget for comfort and for companies that want to arrive looking fresh.</p>

<h3>The 17 seater layout</h3>
<p>The 17 seater Urbania carries up to 17 passengers plus the driver. Our vehicle uses Maharaja recliner captain seats with motorized calf and leg rests, so the seats are individual rather than a shared bench. Each passenger has their own recline, their own leg support, an individual USB fast charger and an individual AC louver above. The cabin also has a mounted Sony Bravia Smart TV, an on-board refrigerator that works as a chiller for cold drinks, ambient blue neon mood lighting, panoramic tinted windows and retractable sunblinds. Luggage goes in a dedicated rear storage area, sized for about 17 bags.</p>
<p>A 17 seater is the largest the Urbania comes in. This is worth stating plainly because people search for a "21 seater Urbania" and are surprised to find none. The platform seats 17 at most. If your group is 18 to 21 people, the right vehicle is the <a href="/fleet/mini-bus/mini-bus-21-seater">21 seater mini bus</a> at ₹34 per km, and for 22 to 25 people the <a href="/fleet/mini-bus/mini-bus-25-seater">25 seater mini bus</a> at ₹38 per km.</p>

<h3>Cabin zones and who should sit where</h3>
<p>A full 17 seater has natural zones, and a little planning makes the journey far better for everyone. Think of the cabin in three parts.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The front zone (nearest the driver).</strong> These seats have the best forward view and the least body movement. Give them to anyone who suffers from motion sickness, to pregnant travellers and to the elderly. Fresh air and a view of the horizon are the best remedies for car sickness, and the front seats offer both.</li>
<li><strong>The middle zone.</strong> The most comfortable ride on the vehicle. Families with small children usually settle here, because the seats are close enough to the aisle for quick access and far enough from the rear wheels to avoid the bumpiest movement.</li>
<li><strong>The rear zone.</strong> The rear seats feel road bumps more, especially on broken ghat sections and speed breakers. They are fine for able-bodied teenagers and adults, and they are where the group usually forms its social centre because of the proximity to the luggage area and to each other.</li>
</ul>
<p>Rotate seats at lunch stops on a long trip. A group that swaps front and rear seats halfway shares the best and worst seats fairly, and avoids the quiet resentment that builds when the same people always sit at the back.</p>

<h3>Luggage planning for a full group</h3>
<p>The rear storage is sized for about 17 bags. That works when the group follows a simple rule: one medium bag per person plus a few soft bags. It stops working when the group arrives with large hard-shell suitcases, wedding trunks, musical instruments or boxes of gifts. Before the day of the trip, count bags, not just people. If you will exceed about 17 bags, tell us in the enquiry so we can confirm what fits or suggest a second vehicle for luggage. A wedding party carrying garments for a ceremony is the classic case: 12 people may have 20 bags, and it is far cheaper to know that on the booking call than at the pickup.</p>
<p>Some practical packing habits make a difference. Pack one small day bag per person that stays inside the cabin with medicines, a water bottle, a phone charger and a light layer, and put the main bag in the rear storage. Elderly travellers should always keep medicines in the day bag, never in the luggage compartment. Fragile items, such as a framed gift or a ceremonial lamp, should travel on a seat, not in the boot.</p>

<h3>How the 17 seater compares with other vehicles</h3>
<p>A group of 13 to 17 people has several sensible options, and the right answer depends on what you value.</p>
