<p>The Tempo Traveller is the lowest-cost way to move 17 people and is a very reasonable choice for short trips and budget pilgrimages. The Urbania costs ₹8 more per km. On a 600 km two-day trip that is a difference of ₹4,800 for the whole vehicle, or about ₹280 per person for a group of 17. Whether that is worth it depends on how long you will sit in the vehicle and who is travelling. For a one-hour airport run, probably not. For a six-hour hill drive with grandparents, most groups say yes.</p>
<p>Two Innova Crystas look cheaper per km, but they are not like for like. Two cars carry 14 people, not 17, so a full group of 17 needs three. Two drivers also means two driver Batas and two sets of tolls, and the group splits in half for the whole journey. On a family trip, that split is the real cost.</p>

<h2>Part 2. Planning Any Large-Group Trip</h2>

<h3>Start with the headcount and the bag count</h3>
<p>Every quotation starts from two numbers: how many people and how many bags. Confirm them with the group before you call. People often say "about 15" and arrive as 18. Ask the group organiser to collect a final list by name, because a name list stops last-minute additions and makes the pickup plan easier. If the number could realistically be 18 or more, plan for the 21 seater mini bus now, rather than discovering the problem at the pickup point.</p>

<h3>Appoint one coordinator</h3>
<p>Large groups run well when one person talks to the driver and to us. That person confirms pickup times, holds the final passenger list, handles toll receipts and decides on stops. Without a coordinator, a vehicle of 17 receives 17 different instructions, and the driver ends up mediating arguments about where to eat. Choose someone organised and a little firm. Share their number with us and with the group.</p>

<h3>Plan the pickups before the day</h3>
<p>A group of 17 rarely lives at one address. Decide whether everyone gathers at a single point or whether the Urbania makes several pickups. Multiple pickups across Bangalore can add an hour or more because of traffic, and every stop adds distance. We pick up from any locality, for example <a href="/urbania-rent-whitefield">Whitefield</a>, <a href="/urbania-rent-koramangala">Koramangala</a>, <a href="/urbania-rent-electronic-city">Electronic City</a>, <a href="/urbania-rent-indiranagar">Indiranagar</a>, <a href="/urbania-rent-hebbal">Hebbal</a> and <a href="/urbania-rent-yelahanka">Yelahanka</a>. For early starts, a single central meeting point is usually faster than a chain of doorstep pickups. For late-evening returns, doorstep drops are easy and worth the extra stops.</p>
<p>Give the driver the exact address, a landmark and a phone number for each pickup. Bangalore addresses can be confusing, and the driver loses far more time looking for a building than driving between areas.</p>

<h3>Choose the departure time with traffic in mind</h3>
<p>Bangalore's traffic changes the length of the first hour of any outstation trip. A departure from Whitefield or Electronic City at 8:30 AM on a weekday means slow movement through IT corridors, while the same trip at 5:00 AM clears the city in half the time. For hill destinations like Ooty, Coorg and Wayanad, an early start also protects daylight for the ghat sections. Duty runs from 6:00 AM to 10:00 PM, and extra driver Bata applies after 10:00 PM, so plan your return so that the vehicle is back before the night duty cut-off whenever possible.</p>

<h3>Build realistic stops into the schedule</h3>
<p>A group of 17 does not stop in five minutes. Figure on 30 to 45 minutes for a breakfast stop and about an hour for lunch, with a short rest stop every two hours. A six-hour drive becomes an eight-hour day once you add this, and that is normal. Tell the group the true arrival time. Groups that promise an arrival by 1:00 PM and arrive at 3:30 PM start the holiday in a bad mood.</p>

<h3>Agree the plan for tolls, parking and permits</h3>
<p>Tolls, parking, interstate permits and state taxes are billed at actuals, and for a long trip they add up. Ask the coordinator to keep the toll receipts and to settle them at the end of the trip. We itemise expected extras in the written quotation so you are not surprised, and any state border permit for trips to Tamil Nadu, Kerala or Goa is listed in advance.</p>

<h2>Part 3. Wedding Transportation with a 17 Seater Urbania</h2>
<p>Weddings are one of the busiest uses of the 17 seater, because a wedding is a logistics problem disguised as a celebration. Guests arrive from different cities, stay at different hotels and need to be at the venue at fixed moments. A single 17 seater Urbania can anchor the transport plan for the immediate family, while other vehicles cover the wider guest list.</p>

<h3>Who to put in the Urbania</h3>
<p>Use the Urbania for the people whose comfort matters most and whose timing is least flexible: grandparents, the bride's or groom's immediate family, elderly relatives and young children. The recliner seats and the quiet cabin make a real difference for older guests during the long ceremony days, and the individual AC louvers let everyone set their own comfort. Use <a href="/fleet/tempo-traveller/tempo-traveller-17-seater">17 seater Tempo Travellers</a> or a <a href="/fleet/mini-bus/mini-bus-25-seater">25 seater mini bus</a> for the wider guest list.</p>

<h3>Build a wedding transport timetable</h3>
<p>Write the timetable down. A typical wedding weekend has five or six movement blocks: arrival at the airport or station, transfer to hotels, hotel to the mehendi or sangeet venue, hotel to the muhurtham, venue to the reception, and departures. For each block write the time, the pickup point, the drop point and the number of people. A one-page table shared with the group coordinator and with us removes most of the stress.</p>
<p>Leave buffers. Wedding timings slip, and nobody enjoys a driver watching the clock. Tell us the actual muhurtham time, not just the start time of the event, so we schedule the vehicle early enough for guests to arrive before the key moment.</p>

<h3>Dress, luggage and decoration</h3>
<p>Wedding groups carry silk sarees, jewellery boxes, garlands and gifts. Keep jewellery with the owner in the cabin, not in the luggage storage. Garments should travel in garment bags laid flat or hung where the vehicle allows, and fresh flower garlands should travel in the cabin with the AC on. If you plan to decorate the vehicle with flowers or ribbons for a baraat or bridal entry, tell us beforehand so the decoration is agreed and does not block the driver's view or damage the vehicle.</p>

<h3>Airport arrivals for wedding guests</h3>
<p>Guests who fly into Kempegowda International Airport are best met by a driver who knows the flight timing. A 17 seater Urbania can collect a whole family at once, which is far easier on a bride's parents than coordinating five separate cabs. See our <a href="/services/airport-transfer">airport transfer service</a> and the <a href="/services/wedding-transportation">wedding transportation service</a> for how we schedule multiple vehicles across one event.</p>

<h2>Part 4. Pilgrimage Trips by 17 Seater Urbania</h2>
<p>Pilgrimages are among the most common long trips for large groups from Bangalore. They combine early starts, long drives, temple queues and late returns, and they often include elderly travellers. A comfortable vehicle changes the experience of the whole journey.</p>

<h3>Tirupati and Tirumala</h3>
<p>Tirupati is about 255 km from Bangalore, a drive of around five and a half to six hours. Groups usually start before dawn so they reach the foothills in time for darshan. Book darshan tickets through the official Tirumala Tirupati Devasthanams channels well before travel, as slots and special entry tickets fill early, and carry the original ID that matches the booking for every pilgrim. Follow the temple's dress code, which asks for traditional attire. Because Tirupati is in Andhra Pradesh, interstate permits and state taxes apply to the vehicle and are itemised in your quotation. Decide beforehand whether the group will walk up the hill or take the road, and plan the vehicle's waiting time accordingly. A 17 seater Urbania suits a full family group, and the recliners help elderly pilgrims recover during the long wait and return drive. See the <a href="/blog/cost/bangalore-to-tirupati-force-urbania">Force Urbania fare guide for Tirupati</a>.</p>

<h3>Mantralaya</h3>
<p>Mantralaya, the town of the Raghavendra Swamy Brindavana on the banks of the Tungabhadra, lies in Andhra Pradesh, a long day's drive from Bangalore. Many groups travel overnight or leave very early and stay one night. Plan accommodation in advance during festival periods, and tell the driver where the group intends to stay so the vehicle can be parked safely. Mantralaya is a popular family trip from Bangalore, and one customer review on our Google profile describes a family of 15 travelling there with us.</p>

<h3>Dharmasthala, Kukke Subramanya and Udupi</h3>
<p>The coastal Karnataka temple circuit, with Dharmasthala, Kukke Subramanya and Udupi, is a popular multi-day trip. The route passes through the Western Ghats, and the Shiradi and Charmadi ghat sections require an experienced driver who understands the weather and the traffic. Monsoon months bring heavy rain and occasional road disruption, so check the conditions before leaving and keep the schedule flexible. A 17 seater Urbania handles a family group on this circuit comfortably and keeps the luggage in one place. See <a href="/services/pilgrimage-tours">pilgrimage tours</a> and our tour package for <a href="/tour-packages/dharmasthala-kukke-pilgrimage">Dharmasthala and Kukke</a>.</p>

<h3>Practical advice for pilgrimage groups</h3>
<ul>
<li>Carry original ID cards for every traveller, since temples and accommodation often ask for them.</li>
<li>Pack a small bag of footwear and wipes for temple stops where shoes are left outside.</li>
<li>Keep medicines, glucose and water within reach of elderly travellers at all times.</li>
<li>Tell us in advance about dietary needs and fasting days so stops can be planned around vegetarian meals.</li>
<li>Agree a rule for the driver's rest. A driver who has driven all night should not drive again at dawn. Plan an overnight stay so the vehicle and the driver rest before the return.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Part 5. Corporate Offsites and Delegations</h2>
<p>Companies use the 17 seater Urbania for team offsites, client visits, leadership retreats and visiting delegations. The vehicle's calm cabin, the Smart TV and the charging at every seat suit a group that wants to work lightly, talk or rest during the drive.</p>

<h3>Why one vehicle beats several cars for a team</h3>
<p>A team in one vehicle arrives together, which means the day starts on time and the group shares the journey. A set of cars arrives in a staggered line, and people who travelled in separate cars miss the conversations that make an offsite worthwhile. For leadership groups, one vehicle also keeps travel costs transparent: one driver, one Bata, one set of tolls and one invoice.</p>

<h3>Invoicing and approvals</h3>
<p>We issue GST invoices for companies. Share your company name, GSTIN and billing address when you enquire so the invoice is correct the first time. If your finance team needs a quotation for approval before booking, ask for the written quotation in advance. It lists the per-km rate, the daily minimum, the Bata and the expected extras, which is the document finance teams usually need. See <a href="/services/corporate-travel">corporate travel</a> for recurring arrangements.</p>

<h3>Offsite destinations that suit a team of up to 17</h3>
<p>Popular corporate destinations from Bangalore include Coorg and Chikmagalur for resort offsites, Mysore for a shorter day trip and Kabini for a wildlife-focused retreat. Resorts in these areas often have limited parking, so tell the resort about the vehicle's size in advance. A 17 seater Urbania is a high-roof van, so it needs the same care at low gates and car park entrances that any large van needs.</p>

<h2>Part 6. Family Reunions and Friends' Holidays</h2>
<p>Not every large trip is a ceremony or a company event. Families that live in different cities meet for reunions, and friend groups plan holidays that are more fun in one vehicle than in a convoy. The 17 seater Urbania is a good fit because everyone can talk, share snacks, watch something on the Smart TV and sing along without shouting between cars.</p>
<p>For multi-generational groups, agree some house rules before the trip. Decide on music volume, on whether the TV is for everyone or only for the children, and on a rough plan for stops. A group that has talked about these things in advance spends the trip enjoying it instead of negotiating it.</p>
<p>For groups with children, bring a small bag of entertainment and a plan for motion sickness. Seat children toward the front or middle, keep the cabin cool and keep snacks light. The retractable sunblinds help young passengers nap, and the individual USB chargers keep tablets running.</p>

